  "notes": "Tuwuli follows standard West African orthography conventions. Digraphs like 'kp', 'gb', 'ny', and 'ts' represent single phonemes and are treated as distinct graphemes. Nasalization is marked with a tilde, and high tone is marked with an acute accent. The letter 'c' is extremely rare (frequency 1) and likely occurs in loanwords or as part of the 'ch' digraph. Vowels with tildes not explicitly in the isolated frequency list but found in the corpus sample (ɛ̃, ɔ̃) have been included for completeness. Nasalisation is handled by the combining tilde in \"diacritics\"; precomposed nasal vowels are therefore not listed as separate graphemes.",
